Introduction
Stiff-man syndrome (SMS) is clinically diagnosed by pro-
gressive rigidity which usually begins in the axial muscles
and progresses to the proximal muscles of the limbs, and
paroxysmal painful spasms precipitated by movement,
sensory stimulation, or emotion, superimposed on the rigid-
ity [22, 29]. Other motor and sensory functions are usu-
ally not affected, and the continuous motor activity in af-
fected muscles is relieved by diazepam [22]. Physiologi-
cal and pharmacological studies have suggested that sup-
pression of g-amino butyric acid (GABA)ergic inhibitory
pathways may be the basis for the a -motor neuron over-
activity which generates the rigidity and spasm in SMS
[27, 37, 42]. Recently, antibodies against glutamic acid
decarboxylase (GAD), the single enzyme catalyzing the
synthesis of GABA, have been demonstrated in patients
with SMS [36]. An autoimmune mechanism was, there-
fore, proposed as the underlying cause of the suppression
of GABAergic inhibitory 
pathways in SMS. However,
pathological studies on GAD- or GABA-containing neu-
rons in SMS patients are extremely scarce and unsatisfac-
tory in the literature [41].
In cats, small neurons in the ventral part of Rexed’s
lamina VII have been established to be GABAergic in-
hibitory neurons [7, 21, 34, 45]. However, their precise
identification in humans remains unresolved. In addition,
quantitative studies regarding the sizes and sizedistribu-
tion of small and intermediate neurons in human spinal
cords are very limited [35]. In SMS, reduction of small
and intermediate neurons in the ventral horn of the spinal
cord, particularly in its ventromedial part, has been de-
scribed [17, 43, 44]. However, a reduction of large neu-
