Discussion
Our study demonstrated that, in the ventral horn of the
spinal cord of the SMS patient, the density of neurons
with a somal area of 500–1500 mm
2 was significantly de-
creased, as was that of neurons with a somal area of
0–500 mm
2. This result indicates that not only interneu-
rons, but also a - or g-motor neurons were involved in the
SMS patient.
There are few studies regarding the correlations between
cell size and cell type in human spinal cord. Using a mor-
phometric method, Kawamura et al. [18, 19] showed three
peaks in a somal diameter-frequency histogram of ventral
spinal neurons in the lumbar segment. They found a rea-
sonably close concordance between the average numbers
of large and intermediate neurons and those of large and
intermediate-caliber myelinated fibers in the ventral
spinal roots. On the basis of these results, they concluded
that large neurons (33–59 mm in diameter) are a -motor
neurons, intermediate neurons (24–33 mm) are g-motor
neurons and those with a small diameter (< 23 mm) are in-
terneurons. Applying these values to the simple regres-
sion in Fig. 4, the ventral horn neurons in our study were
classified as follows; a -motor neurons were larger than
1000 mm
2 in somal area, g-motor neurons were approxi-
mately 500–1000 mm2 and interneurons were smaller than
500 mm2.
Howell et al. [15] emphasized a selective reduction in
the number of small and intermediate neurons smaller
than 30 mm in diameter in a SMS patient; however, the
data presented in the paper showed that the number of
larger neurons of 30–46 mm in diameter was also de-
creased. In addition, the patient in that study had neuro-
genic muscular atrophy. In our SMS patient, in addition to
the 27% reduction in the density of interneurons with a
somal area less than 500 mm
2, we found a 70% overall re-
duction of smaller a -motor neurons with a somal area of
1000–1500 mm2 and a 33% reduction in the density of g-
motor neurons, with a somal area of 500–1000 mm2. Thus,
the reduction in the density of smaller a -motor neurons
was more than twofold compared to that of the g-motor
neurons or interneurons. A dying-back neuropathy, which
occasionally occurs related to an intensive therapy, may
damage spinal motor neurons. Experimentally, neurons are
reported to disappear within 5 weeks or much later after
axonal damage [38]. In our patient, the lower truncal and
gluteal muscles as well as those of proximal lower limbs
showed spasms and subsequently developed atrophy
within 2 weeks. No sensory disturbance was noted. This
clinical presentation was highly unlikely for dying-back
neuropathy, which is a distal dominant sensorimotor neu-
ropathy [4]. At autopsy, the distribution of muscle atrophy
corresponds to the level of spinal ventral horns involved
and neurogenic atrophy was confirmed in the muscles.
We, thus, considered neuronal loss in the patient to be in-
trinsic to the disease, i.e., SMS per se. As previously de-
scribed [2, 15, 28], we also found central chromatolytic
changes in large motor neurons. The chromatolytic
changes may be reflected in the relatively increased den-
sity of the larger a -motor neurons, with a somal area of
over 2500 mm
2 as shown in Fig. 5. These findings indicate
the involvement of not only interneurons but also of a - or
g-motor neurons in SMS. Morphological studies of rat
spinal cord revealed that GABAergic neurons were found
in all the Rexed’s laminae except lamina IX [3]; in con-
trast, a - and g-motor neurons were interspersed in Rexed’s
lamina IX [5, 9, 30], indicating that a - and g-motor neu-
rons are not GABAergic. Furthermore, all GABAergic
neurons are smaller than 500 mm
2 in somal area and are,
thus, considered to belong to interneurons [3]. From our
findings and these reports, pathological processes in SMS
may involve more than just GABAergic inhibitory path-
ways. Since GABAergic interneurons constitute only a
small proportion of spinal interneurons [3], the reduction
in the density of spinal interneurons probably accounts for
the reduction in the number of non-GABAergic interneu-
rons more than that of GABAergic neurons.
On light microscopy, immunoreactivity of GAD in rat
spinal cord is seen as punctate reaction products in both
ventral and dorsal horns. On electron microscopy, the im-
munoproducts correspond to GAD-containing presynaptic
terminals [25, 33]. Since GAD is the single enzyme cat-
alyzing the synthesis of GABA, GAD-like immunoreac-
tivity is considered to represent GABA-like immunoreac-
tivity. Thus, the GAD-positive granules in the neuropil
and around the soma are likely to represent GABA in a
synaptic terminal. We demonstrated that the density and
staining pattern of GAD-positive granules in the SMS pa-
tient were not different from those in controls. This result
indicates that the GABA itself in the synaptic terminals
may be spared in SMS, but does not necessarily indicate
that GABAergic synaptic conduction is intact in SMS.
The GABA receptor or ligands binding to the GABA re-
ceptor must be studied to understand the mechanism of
suppression of GABAergic inhibitory pathways in SMS.
It has been reported that anti-GAD antibody was de-
tected in the serum of only 31–67% of SMS patients [13,
26]. It is uncertain, however, whether SMS in patients
with anti-GAD antibodies is identical to SMS in those
without these antibodies. GAD is distributed over a wide
area of the central nervous system in primates [1, 10, 23].
The substantia nigra or pallidum is known to contain the
highest amount of GAD or GABA [1, 23, 32]. Purkinje
cells also contain high amounts of GAD [31]. Neuronal
